# Search parameters for the self-driving `auto-optimize` command — tracked
# in git, like run-config.env. Edit, commit, `git pull` on the droplet, then
# `sudo systemctl start eval-auto-optimize` to kick off a fresh search.
#
# This is NOT `enable`d at boot and NOT auto-restarted on failure or
# completion (see eval-auto-optimize.service) — it's a one-off, bounded
# search you trigger by hand, not an always-on service like eval-harness.
#
# Same flags as `python3 scripts/eval_harness.py auto-optimize` — see
# eval/README.md. MUST stay double-quoted — see run-config.env's comment
# for why (this file is bash-`source`d too, not systemd's EnvironmentFile
# format).
#
# --baseline-run here is used ONLY by the systemd unit (eval-auto-optimize.service),
# which sources this file directly. The web dashboard's "Launch more rounds"
# button does NOT read this value first — dashboard_server.py's
# _resolve_baseline_for_launch() tries the last search's own recorded state,
# then the run that tested the live current prompt version, and only falls
# back to this file as a last resort (surfaced explicitly in the launch
# response when it does). Real incident this staged fallback was built for:
# this value sat at "auto-r1-v15" — a run from early testing — for the
# entire span of a since-84-version-long optimization lineage, and nothing
# caught it until a human noticed the trajectory didn't match. If you run
# the systemd unit directly, treat this value as needing a manual check
# first: `python3 -c "from scripts.eval import prompts; print(prompts.current_version())"`
# and set --baseline-run to whichever run tested that version.
#
# --baseline-run must be a run-id that already has completed results (e.g.
# do-batch-2) — auto-optimize learns from that run's actual validator/judge
# failures, it doesn't generate a baseline itself.
AUTO_OPTIMIZE_ARGS="--baseline-run do-batch-2 --rounds 5 --concurrency 6 --time-budget-minutes 60"
